.TH MAN 1 "April 19, 1988"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
man \- print out the manual
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.B man
[
.B \-
] [
.B -a
] [
.B \-M
.I path
] [
section
]
title ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fIMan\fP is the program which provides on-line access to the UNIX manual.
If a \fIsection\fP specifier is given, \fIman\fP looks in that section of
the manual for the given \fItitle\fP(s).  \fISection\fP is an Arabic
section number ('3' for example).
If \fIsection\fP is omitted, \fIman\fP searches all sections of the manual,
giving preference to commands over library subroutines, and displays the
first manual page it finds.
If the \fI-a\fP option is supplied,
\fIman\fP displays all applicable manual pages.
.PP
Normally \fIman\fP checks in standard locations (/usr/man, /usr/new/man,
/usr/local/man)
for manual information.  This can be changed by supplying a search path
with the \fB-M\fP flag.  The search path is a colon
(':') separated list of directories in which \fIman\fP expects to find
standard manual subdirectory structure (e.g. cat3).  This search path can 
also be set with the environmental variable \fIMANPATH\fP.
.PP
If the standard output is a teletype, and the \fB-\fP flag is not provided,
\fIman\fP uses \fImore\fP(1), or the pager provided by the environmental
variable \fIPAGER\fP, to display the manual page.
.PP
The FORTRAN version of section 3 of the manual may be specified by using
the \fIsection\fP '3f'.
.SH FILES
.nf
.ta \w'/usr/new/man/man?   'u
/usr/man/cat?	standard formatted manual pages
/usr/new/man/cat?	additional standard formatted pages
/usr/local/man/cat?	local formatted pages
/usr/src/man	standard unformatted manual pages
.fi
.SH SEE\ ALSO
apropos(1), more(1), whatis(1), whereis(1)
.SH BUGS
The manual is supposed to be reproducible either on the
phototypesetter or on a typewriter, however, on a typewriter,
some information is necessarily lost.
